1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,331
Here's the rubric equivalent of last lecture's line: reviewed by a subject matter

2
00:00:05,331 --> 00:00:06,499
expert for quality.

3
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:08,344
It sounds like a real process.

4
00:00:08,344 --> 00:00:13,447
It passes review the same way "helpful and accurate" did, back in the first lecture

5
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:14,431
of this section.

6
00:00:14,431 --> 00:00:16,214
The problem isn't the expert.

7
00:00:16,214 --> 00:00:21,010
The problem is that "quality" was never written down anywhere two people could

8
00:00:21,010 --> 00:00:21,502
compare.

9
00:00:21,502 --> 00:00:26,483
Ask that same expert to score the same output twice, a week apart, and there's no

10
00:00:26,483 --> 00:00:28,881
guarantee they land on the same answer.

11
00:00:28,881 --> 00:00:31,218
Would they even agree with themselves?

12
00:00:31,218 --> 00:00:34,661
Ask a different expert, and the bar quietly moves again.

13
00:00:34,661 --> 00:00:39,642
Whoever happens to be reviewing that Tuesday is, functionally, the entire rubric.

14
00:00:39,642 --> 00:00:44,807
This is the same failure as the untestable acceptance criterion from earlier in this

15
00:00:44,807 --> 00:00:48,742
section, just wearing a reviewer's name instead of an adjective.

16
00:00:48,742 --> 00:00:52,186
"Helpful and accurate" hid its vagueness in the wording.

17
00:00:52,186 --> 00:00:57,166
"Reviewed by an expert" hides the same vagueness behind a person, which is why it

18
00:00:57,166 --> 00:00:59,195
survives review even more easily.

19
00:00:59,195 --> 00:01:03,807
Nobody wants to question whether the expert knows quality when they see it.

20
00:01:03,807 --> 00:01:07,005
The document just never asked them to write it down.

21
00:01:07,555 --> 00:01:12,349
Same intention again, and only one version can actually be enforced by someone who

22
00:01:12,349 --> 00:01:14,629
wasn't in the room when it was written.

23
00:01:14,629 --> 00:01:17,786
The second version points at a document, not a person.

24
00:01:17,786 --> 00:01:22,697
Anyone can open it, read the six questions, and understand exactly what "good" means

25
00:01:22,697 --> 00:01:23,691
for this feature.

26
00:01:23,691 --> 00:01:28,543
And it doesn't just claim the rubric is good, it names the test that proved it: two

27
00:01:28,543 --> 00:01:33,279
reviewers, agreeing at least eighty-five percent of the time, on cases neither of

28
00:01:33,279 --> 00:01:34,799
them discussed beforehand.

29
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:38,190
Notice what that number is actually doing in the sentence.

30
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:39,359
It isn't decoration.

31
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:44,094
It's evidence that the rubric survived contact with a second person who wasn't in

32
00:01:44,094 --> 00:01:45,790
the room when it was written.

33
00:01:45,790 --> 00:01:50,701
A rubric that's never been read cold by anyone but its author hasn't earned the word

34
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:51,461
"rubric" yet.

35
00:01:51,461 --> 00:01:53,799
It's still a draft with good intentions.

36
00:01:54,349 --> 00:01:59,364
Five questions again, and the same discipline as last lecture: none of these ask you

37
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:01,752
to actually write the six questions yet.

38
00:02:01,752 --> 00:02:06,587
Section four walks through exactly how to do that, and how to run the calibration

39
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:08,199
check that proves it works.

40
00:02:08,199 --> 00:02:10,766
The pass or fail question deserves a pause.

41
00:02:10,766 --> 00:02:15,602
Remember the threshold sentence from two lectures back: score four or five on the

42
00:02:15,602 --> 00:02:16,557
accuracy rubric.

43
00:02:16,557 --> 00:02:21,094
That only means something if the rubric has score bands written down, and if

44
00:02:21,094 --> 00:02:25,750
everyone agrees which bands count as passing before a single case gets scored.

45
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:30,347
Decide that after you've seen the results, and you haven't measured anything.

46
00:02:30,347 --> 00:02:33,272
You've just picked the answer you already wanted.

47
00:02:33,272 --> 00:02:38,287
The versioning question matters for a reason specific to rubrics, not just datasets.

48
00:02:38,287 --> 00:02:43,301
A rubric that quietly changes between one release and the next makes every pass-rate

49
00:02:43,301 --> 00:02:48,196
comparison meaningless, because you'd be measuring against a different bar without

50
00:02:48,196 --> 00:02:49,808
anyone announcing it moved.

51
00:02:49,808 --> 00:02:54,405
Treat an edit to the rubric the same way you'd treat an edit to the threshold

52
00:02:54,405 --> 00:02:56,972
itself: worth a note, a date, and a reason.

53
00:02:57,522 --> 00:03:02,433
Say the honest caveat, because this is the mistake teams make right after they've

54
00:03:02,433 --> 00:03:04,313
done everything else correctly.

55
00:03:04,313 --> 00:03:09,103
Two reviewers disagree on a case, and the instinct is to escalate: let the most

56
00:03:09,103 --> 00:03:11,528
senior person in the room make the call.

57
00:03:11,528 --> 00:03:15,469
That feels efficient, and it's the same trap wearing new clothes.

58
00:03:15,469 --> 00:03:20,502
It replaces an ambiguous question with an authority's opinion, which is exactly the

59
00:03:20,502 --> 00:03:23,473
setup this whole lecture opened by warning about.

60
00:03:23,473 --> 00:03:26,747
The right answer to a disagreement isn't a tiebreaker.

61
00:03:26,747 --> 00:03:31,476
It's rewording the question those two reviewers actually disagreed on, the way

62
00:03:31,476 --> 00:03:36,570
Section four's calibration loop does it, so the next hundred reviewers don't hit the

63
00:03:36,570 --> 00:03:37,176
same fork.

64
00:03:37,176 --> 00:03:40,935
A senior reviewer can absolutely help find the better wording.

65
00:03:40,935 --> 00:03:42,754
They shouldn't be the wording.

66
00:03:42,754 --> 00:03:47,423
Put it in the PRD explicitly if you want it to actually happen under deadline

67
00:03:47,423 --> 00:03:47,969
pressure.

68
00:03:47,969 --> 00:03:52,819
"Disagreements are resolved by rewording the question, not by escalation" is one

69
00:03:52,819 --> 00:03:57,912
sentence, and it's the sentence that stops a rushed launch from quietly reverting to

70
00:03:57,912 --> 00:03:59,974
whoever's most senior in the room.

71
00:04:00,524 --> 00:04:05,401
That's the shape again: a paragraph, not the whole instrument, and every clause in

72
00:04:05,401 --> 00:04:08,077
it something a reviewer could actually check.

73
00:04:08,077 --> 00:04:09,624
Do the exercise literally.

74
00:04:09,624 --> 00:04:14,025
Search your PRD for the word "quality" or "good", and see what defines it.

75
00:04:14,025 --> 00:04:18,902
If you land on a role instead of a rubric, you've found this lecture's gap in your

76
00:04:18,902 --> 00:04:21,162
own document, not just in the example.

77
00:04:21,162 --> 00:04:25,206
It's worth doing this search even on PRDs you didn't write yourself.

78
00:04:25,206 --> 00:04:30,024
The pattern is common enough that finding it isn't a criticism of whoever drafted

79
00:04:30,024 --> 00:04:30,797
the document.

80
00:04:30,797 --> 00:04:35,793
It's just what happens by default, everywhere, until someone deliberately closes the

81
00:04:35,793 --> 00:04:37,339
gap this lecture is about.

82
00:04:37,889 --> 00:04:39,724
One thing before you move on.

83
00:04:39,724 --> 00:04:44,912
Find the place in your PRD where quality gets decided, and check whether it points

84
00:04:44,912 --> 00:04:46,431
at a rubric or a person.

85
00:04:46,431 --> 00:04:49,467
Most, on a first honest look, point at a person.

86
00:04:49,467 --> 00:04:53,390
Then write down, specifically, how disagreement gets resolved.

87
00:04:53,390 --> 00:04:58,325
If your answer was a role rather than a process, that's the fix to make before

88
00:04:58,325 --> 00:04:59,717
anyone starts scoring.

89
00:04:59,717 --> 00:05:04,905
Next lecture takes the dataset and the rubric you've now committed to and asks the

90
00:05:04,905 --> 00:05:10,030
harder question: what happens when the feature fails anyway, and how much of that

91
00:05:10,030 --> 00:05:13,446
failure a human has to catch before it reaches a user.
